  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Company $A$ supplies $80%$ of widgets for a car shop and only $1%$ of their widgets turn out to be defective. Company $B$ supplies the remaining $20%$ of widgets. The probability that a widget from the car shop will be defective is $0.014.$ If a customer randomly purchases a widget supplied by company $B,$ what is the probability that it will be defective?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that a widget will be defective, and let $A$ be the event that the widget is supplied by company $A.$ Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the widget is supplied by company $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly purchased widget will be defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since company $A$ supplies $80%$ of widgets for a car shop and company $B$ the remaining $20%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.80,\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=0.20.\n$$\n\n- Since $1%$ of the widget supplied by company $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a widget from the car shop is defective will be $0.014,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.014.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|{A}^{\u2032}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.014 & =(0.01)(0.80)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})(0.20) \\\\ 0.014 & =0.008+0.20P(D|{A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.20P(D|{A}^{\u2032}) & =0.006 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{\u2032}) & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected widget supplied by company $B$ will be defective is $0.03.$",
